BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[US-Asia Technology Management Center] Mine Less\, Last Longer? Si
 ngapore Solutions for Critical Materials in Energy and Electronics
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261022T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261022T175000
DTSTAMP:20261021T175617Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54179181594470-54179187456103@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Lithium\, cobalt\, gallium and the rare earth elements underpi
 n both the energy transition and modern electronics\, yet their supply cha
 ins are concentrated\, carbon-intensive and increasingly shaped by geopoli
 tics. For a resource-scarce city-state like Singapore that makes 1 in 10 o
 f the world’s semiconductor chips\, the question is less where to mine a
 nd more of how we keep critical materials in use for longer.\n\nDr. Richar
 d Dasher\, Director of the US-ATMC\, will moderate the session.\n\nAbout o
 ur spea\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://asia.stanford.edu/course/topics
 -in-international-technology-management/2026-critical-materials/overview/
 \nLast synced: 2026-10-21 10:56 PDT
LOCATION:Bishop Auditorium
URL:https://asia.stanford.edu/course/topics-in-international-technology-ma
 nagement/2026-critical-materials/overview/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Center for East Asian Studies / History Department] The Human Fac
 tor: Work as Science in Modern China
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261022T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261022T183000
DTSTAMP:20261021T175617Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53764263973882-53764263973883@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Modern industry has never depended on machines alone. It has a
 lso relied on workers who could read uncertain signals\, coordinate hand a
 nd eye motions\, and adapt to changing conditions\, all in the interest of
  keeping production moving. This talk traces how Chinese psychologists mad
 e that “human factor” an object of scientific study across the twentie
 th century its changing political regimes. Beginning with factory investig
 ations and suggestion systems in the 1930s\, it follows industrial psychol
 og\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-10-21 10:56 PDT
LOCATION:Lathrop Library
END:VEVENT
